TEMPERATURE CONTROLLED FAN USING ARDUINO UNO
Abstract:
A Temperature Controlled Fan is an automatic cooling system that adjusts the speed of a DC fan based on the surrounding temperature. The project uses an Arduino Uno microcontroller, an LM35 temperature sensor, and an L293D motor driver. The LM35 continuously measures the ambient temperature and sends the data to the Arduino. According to the measured temperature, the Arduino controls the fan speed using PWM (Pulse Width Modulation). This system reduces power consumption, improves cooling efficiency, and eliminates the need for manual fan control. It is suitable for homes, offices, computer cabinets, laboratories, and industrial environments.
Chapter 1: Introduction
Temperature control is important in homes, industries, and electronic equipment. Conventional fans run at a constant speed even when cooling is not required, resulting in unnecessary energy consumption.
This project automatically controls the fan speed according to the room temperature. When the temperature rises, the fan rotates faster. When the temperature decreases, the fan slows down or stops.
The project is simple, low-cost, energy-efficient, and easy to implement using Arduino technology.
Chapter 2: Problem Statement
Traditional fans require manual adjustment, leading to:
Increased electricity consumption
User inconvenience
Inefficient cooling
Continuous operation even at low temperatures
The proposed system overcomes these problems by automatically controlling the fan speed.
